# .gitattributes — what a byte means, decided once (2026-09-01)
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS, and why it is dated the day before the first commit. This
# tree grew on Windows with mixed endings: most sources are LF, a handful
# (app.cpp, email.cpp, site.cpp, builder.cpp, find_long.py) are CRLF. Nothing
# enforced either, so an ordinary `sed -i` silently rewrote all 1866 lines of
# render/site.cpp from CRLF to LF while changing two of them. That was caught
# by eye. After the first push the same slip is a 1866-line diff in somebody
# else's review, every time, forever.
#
# `text=auto` settles it: git stores text as LF and checks it out however the
# platform wants. The working tree keeps behaving the way Windows expects; the
# repository stops carrying the question.

* text=auto

# ── FILES WHOSE ENDINGS ARE PART OF THEIR CONTRACT ──────────────────────────
# Shell scripts are executed by bash (tests/headless_smoke.sh is a ctest case,
# tools/golden_render.sh is the render golden). A CR at the end of a `then` or
# a heredoc terminator is a syntax error, so these are LF wherever they land.
*.sh        text eol=lf

# The Windows launcher is read by cmd.exe, which wants CRLF.
*.bat       text eol=crlf

# ── EXPLICIT TEXT, so nothing here is ever guessed at ────────────────────────
*.c         text
*.h         text
*.cpp       text
*.hpp       text
*.py        text
*.md        text
*.txt       text
*.json      text
*.css       text
*.js        text
*.cmake     text
CMakeLists.txt text
*.sha256    text
*.voidscript text

# ── BINARY, never touched, never diffed as text ─────────────────────────────
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.png       binary
*.ttf       binary
*.woff2     binary
*.pdf       binary
*.a         binary
*.db        binary
*.miga      binary
*.ico       binary
